course: dictionary
lesson: 1806
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. «The big companies have a lot of clout.» Here clout is not about hitting anything. The clue is big companies, so clout means power to get your way.
explain: «He got a clout round the ear for being rude.» Now clout means a hard hit with the hand. The clues are ear and rude. Clout can also be a verb: «The batter clouts the ball over the fence» means hits it very hard. Check by swapping in your meaning: hitting fits, but power does not.
explain: Old sayings can use words in ways we no longer use them. «Ne'er cast a clout till May be out» is an old English saying. Here a clout is a piece of cloth, and the saying means do not take off your warm clothes too early in spring. The rest of the saying gives you the clue.
fact: Clout means power or influence, as in «have clout».
fact: Clout can mean a hard hit, as in «a clout round the ear».
fact: Clout can also be a verb, meaning to hit someone or something hard.
fact: In sport, clout means a hard hit of the ball, such as a home run or a six.
fact: Clout can be an old word for a piece of cloth, as in «Ne'er cast a clout till May be out». Hitting people is not okay today, so if someone hurts you, tell a grown-up.
exercise: «The big companies have a lot of clout.» What does clout mean here? | *Power to get their way | A hard hit with the hand | A piece of cloth | A loud noise
exercise: The batter «clouts» the ball over the fence. What does clouts mean? | Drops it gently | Counts the runs | *Hits it very hard | Holds it still
exercise: In the old saying «Ne'er cast a clout till May be out», what is a clout? | *A piece of cloth | A home run | A hard hit | Power over others
test: «Their financial clout meant the firm could buy the whole town.» What does financial clout suggest? | Losing a game | A piece of cloth | Being hit hard with money | *Money power to get their way
test: Ayo says clout always means hitting. Is Ayo right? | Yes, it always means a hard hit | *No, it can also mean power or influence | No, it only appears in old sayings | Yes, but only in sport
exam: In a news report, «have clout» most likely means | Is wrapped in cloth | Has scored a home run | Has been hit hard | *Has power to get their way
